        {
          "path": "_m/s/w/e/q/temple-of-athena-polias-priene-wp/the-temple-of-athena-polias-pr-1.jpg",
          "filename": "The Temple of Athena Polias, Priene, Turkey (8395514280).jpg",
          "caption": "The five columns were erected in 1965–66 from rubble and are 3 metres (9.8 ft) short of the calculated original column height.\n\nBegun ca. 340 B.C.; dedicated by Alexander the Great in 334 B.C., when the east end of the temple was completed at least up to the anta. The date of completion of the entire temple is uncertain - some have detected two distinct building phases, the latter falling in the mid-second century, while others maintain that the temple was substantially complete by the last quarter of the fourth century B.C. The cult statue, a version of the Athena Parthenos, was not installed until ca. 158 -156 B.C., and was probably dedicated by the Cappadocian ruler Orophernes. After 27 B.C., the sanctuary was rededicated to Athena Polias and Augustus, and continued as an important cult center throughout the Imperial period. The transformation of the opisthodomos into a closed space - perhaps a small treasury - may belong to this Roman phase; other scholars date the moldings of the new door of the opisthodomos to the second century B.C., that is, to a second phase of construction. The temple was used as a church in the Byzantine period.",
          "alt": "The five columns were erected in 1965–66 from rubble and are 3 metres (9.8 ft) short of the calculated original column height.\n\nBegun ca. 340 B.C.; dedicated by Alexander the Great in 334 B.C., when the east end of the temple was completed at least up to the anta. The date of completion of the entire temple is uncertain - some have detected two distinct building phases, the latter falling in the mid-second century, while others maintain that the temple was substantially complete by the last quarter of the fourth century B.C. The cult statue, a version of the Athena Parthenos, was not installed until ca. 158 -156 B.C., and was probably dedicated by the Cappadocian ruler Orophernes. After 27 B.C., the sanctuary was rededicated to Athena Polias and Augustus, and continued as an important cult center throughout the Imperial period. The transformation of the opisthodomos into a closed space - perhaps a small treasury - may belong to this Roman phase; other scholars date the moldings of the new door of the opisthodomos to the second century B.C., that is, to a second phase of construction. The temple was used as a church in the Byzantine period.",
          "width": 4179,
          "height": 3132,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
          "artist": "Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Temple_of_Athena_Polias,_Priene,_Turkey_(8395514280).jpg",
